                <p>Account suspension means interrupted ad delivery, wasted budget, and potential business disruption. Even more frustrating is submitting a weak appeal that gets rejected immediately. Here are five high-frequency questions to help you navigate the Google Ads appeal process.</p>
<h2>What Are the Common Reasons for Account Suspension?</h2>
<p>Before preparing your appeal, you need to understand the root cause. Google suspensions typically fall into three categories:</p>
<p><strong>Policy violations</strong>: Promoting prohibited products/services, misleading claims, clickbait tactics<br />
<strong>Payment issues</strong>: Credit card chargebacks, billing address mismatches, outstanding payments<br />
<strong>Suspicious traffic</strong>: Sudden click spikes, bot traffic, abnormal投放script activity</p>
<p>Payment-related suspensions are usually easier to resolve, while policy violations require more careful documentation.</p>
<h2>What Basic Documents Do You Need Before Filing an Appeal?</h2>
<p>Submitting an incomplete appeal wastes your only chance. Prepare the following checklist in advance:</p>
<ul>
<li>Business license or company registration certificate (must match account information)</li>
<li>Proof of website ownership (DNS records or backend screenshots)</li>
<li>Recent account dashboard screenshots showing account structure and spending data</li>
<li>Screenshots of violation notifications or original email content</li>
<li>Product compliance documentation from third-party platforms (if applicable)</li>
</ul>
<p>For accounts with multiple time zones or sub-accounts, prepare an account hierarchy diagram to help reviewers quickly understand your business model.</p>
<h2>How to Write an Effective Appeal Statement?</h2>
<p>This is the most overlooked part. Many marketers simply write "Please restore our account," which will never work.</p>
<p><strong>An effective appeal includes three key elements:</strong></p>
<p><strong>1. Acknowledge the issue</strong> – Clearly state the violation without trying to downplay or shift blame<br />
<strong>2. Explain the cause</strong> – Was it an internal operation error, third-party agency issue, or policy misunderstanding?<br />
<strong>3. Detail corrective measures</strong> – What have you done already, and what will you do to prevent recurrence? Include landing page revisions, strategy adjustments, and improved review processes</p>
<p>A practical example: A cross-border e-commerce team had their site flagged for exaggerated efficacy claims. Their appeal included screenshots of revised pages and documented their new weekly content review process. The account was reinstated within 3 days.</p>
<h2>How Long Does the Appeal Review Take?</h2>
<p>Google's official guidance is 3-5 business days, but actual wait times vary significantly:</p>
